<h1>Is it better to be a generalist or specialist when you have no experience?</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">Specialist. Without a track record, breadth reads as desperation while depth reads as proof, because companies do not hire "quick learners" when they can hire someone who has already done the exact thing they need done. The generalist pitch is a luxury for the already-employed.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>53% of employers have dropped degree requirements, up from 30% the year before, but they replaced them with specific skill tests, not open-mindedness about potential. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla State of Skills-Based Hiring 2025</a></li>
    <li>Resume use fell to 67%, down from 73% in 2024, recruiters increasingly bypass the document and go straight to work samples. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla State of Skills-Based Hiring 2025</a></li>
    <li>The "degree reset" found 46% of employers dropped degree requirements for some roles, but only 31% followed through with real change, and fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected it. <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Burning Glass Institute + HBS</a></li>
    <li>An internship cuts the odds of underemployment by 48.5%, not because it proves you can do anything, but because it proves you can do one specific thing in a real context. <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">Strada Institute + Burning Glass Institute, "Talent Disrupted"</a></li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>Why does the generalist pitch fail for unproven candidates?</h2>
  <p>The generalist argument sounds reasonable: you are flexible, adaptable, a "blank slate" the company can mold. This works if you already have credibility. A former Google PM can say "I can do anything" and people believe it because the credential vouches for the underlying ability. You do not have this.</p>
  <p>Without experience, the generalist framing creates a signaling problem where the recruiter reads "I can do a little of everything" as "I have done nothing well enough to mention," the resume equivalent of a restaurant advertising "we serve food." The market for unproven labor is not looking for potential, it is looking for de-risked execution on a specific pain point.</p>
  <p>The skills-based shift has made this more brutal, not less. Employers dropped degree requirements to hire faster and more precisely, not to open the gates to untested generalists. They replaced one filter with a tighter one: prove you can do the exact task in the job description. Breadth does not survive that filter.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What does "specialist" mean when you have literally done nothing?</h2>
  <p>It does not mean ten years of expertise. It means directional proof: one or two specific outputs that sit at the intersection of what you can do now and what a specific role actually requires. A marketing coordinator does not need to have run campaigns for Nike. They need to have run <em>a</em> campaign, measured it, and can talk through the numbers.</p>
  <p>The specialist without experience builds this proof deliberately. They pick a narrow lane (email marketing for SaaS, not "marketing"), learn the specific tools (Klaviyo, not "CRM software"), and produce a portfolio piece that mimics the real job: a sequence of three emails with subject lines, send times, and projected open rates based on industry benchmarks. This is not fake expertise but a simulation of expertise, which is exactly what the employer is testing for in a skills-based screen.</p>
  <p>The alternative is the generalist trap: you learn "marketing" broadly, you list "social media, content, SEO, analytics" on your resume, and you apply to everything. Each application is weaker than the last because none of them show a recruiter a single thing they can say yes to.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How narrow should you go?</h2>
  <p>Narrow enough that you can name the exact person who would hire you tomorrow. Not an industry. Not a function. A role, at a company size, with a specific headache. "I help early-stage B2B SaaS companies reduce churn through onboarding email sequences" is a specialist pitch. "I do marketing" is a generalist pitch. The first one gets a conversation with the founder who just watched 30% of free trials disappear in week one.</p>
  <p>This feels counterintuitive because it closes doors. You are explicitly not applying to 90% of jobs. But those doors were never open. The generalist applies to 200 jobs and hears back on 3. The specialist applies to 30 and hears back on 8. The math is not about volume, it is about relevance density.</p>
  <p>The founder thesis applies here directly: the old system (the filter OS) rewards breadth because the degree is a general credential that says "I survived a broad curriculum." The new system rewards depth because the recruiter is not filtering for survival, they are matching for a specific scored capability. zero builds this by putting you in company-shaped scenarios where you produce scored work in a narrow lane, not by certifying you as generally competent.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>When does generalist strategy ever make sense?</h2>
  <p>At the very top of the labor market, in roles where the job itself is undefined. Early employees at startups sometimes need to be "the person who figures things out." But these roles go to people who have already figured things out somewhere else. The generalist premium exists only after you have paid the specialist tax to get in.</p>
  <p>There is also a pivot case: you have specialist proof in one area and want to move to another. The move is still specialist-to-specialist, with a narrative bridge ("I ran paid acquisition for e-commerce, now I am applying that funnel thinking to B2B demand gen"). It is never "I did a bit of everything, so I can do your thing too."</p>
  <p>The honest frame: generalist is a career stage, not a career strategy. You become one after you have been several specialists, not at the starting line.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How do you build specialist proof with no job, no internship, no network?</h2>
  <p>You simulate the job until the simulation becomes indistinguishable from the job. This is the core mechanism zero is built on: company-shaped scenarios with real decisions, real outputs, and scoring against a professional bar. Not a course that teaches you about email marketing. A scenario where you write the emails, set the segments, and get scored on open rate projections and list hygiene decisions.</p>
  <p>The output is not a certificate. It is a submission with feedback: what you did, what a professional would have done differently, and a score that a recruiter can read. This is the structural replacement for the degree-as-filter. Instead of "I graduated, trust me," it is "I did this, here is how I scored, here is what I fixed."</p>
  <p>The specialist without experience has one path: produce work in a narrow lane, get it scored, show the score. Everything else is theater.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Can I be a specialist in two things?</h3>
    <p>No. Two narrow lanes reads as one confused lane to a recruiter. Pick the intersection if it is genuinely one role ("technical SEO for JavaScript frameworks"), but do not present two separate specializations. Depth requires sacrifice.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>What if I do not know what to specialize in?</h3>
    <p>Pick the intersection of what you have done anything in and what has entry-level demand. Not what you love, not what pays most. What you have a marginal advantage in and what hires. You can pivot after you have proof; you cannot pivot from nothing.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Does specializing limit my long-term career?</h3>
    <p>It limits your first job search, which is the bottleneck. Specialists move faster into generalist roles than generalists move into any role. The specialist has proof of execution, which transfers across domains. The generalist has proof of nothing.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>How do I explain my specialization in interviews without sounding inexperienced?</h3>
    <p>Talk about the work, not the absence of work. "Here is the campaign I built, here is the metric I targeted, here is what I would change." The interviewer will fill in the experience gap if you demonstrate competence. They cannot fill in competence if you demonstrate flexibility.</p>
  </div>
</section>

